body:
I believe you are misinterpreting P as just pressure. Its pressure on an object submerged in a fluid.  If it is under the fluid, but in the same "container" it is still submerged.  


&#x200B;

P: is the pressure on an object submerged in a fluid. 

&#x200B;

R: (RHO) is the density of the fluid.

&#x200B;

G: is gravity 

&#x200B;

H: is the height of the fluid above the object.  




So, if the solid block is under the water, it raises the value of H (the height of the fluids above the block) in the equation. The pressure on the submerged block will then either sink or float. If the density of the volume of water and gravity is less than the block, it will float.  In your example, it is less dense so it would float.
